WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.650
A joint is a crack with little or no shear displacement

00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:07.430
along its surface. A fault is a fracture across which rocks

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:10.650
have slipped. Follow a matching bed or other marker across the

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:12.670
break before deciding whether movement occurred.

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:16.930
Joints can open as rocks cool and contract, as overlying rock

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:20.650
is removed and pressure decreases, or under tectonic stresses.

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:23.070
Columnar joints form during cooling.

00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:28.330
Sheet-like unloading joints follow reduced confinement as deeply formed rock approaches

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:28.910
the surface.

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:33.950
An opening-mode joint is oriented approximately perpendicular to the direction in

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:35.630
which the rock is pulled apart.

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:39.170
Joint sets can divide an outcrop into regular blocks.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:43.150
Identify the opening direction and the visible planes before deciding which

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:43.950
stresses acted.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:48.690
Differential stress can produce a fracture along which blocks slip.

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:52.370
The fault orientation depends on the full stress field, including stresses

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:56.190
in the other directions. An observed fracture alone does not establish

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:59.430
the amount or sense of motion without a displaced marker or

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:00.230
other evidence.
